    {
      "id": 116,
      "type": "MarkdownNote",
      "pos": [
        -2030,
        4850
      ],
      "size": [
        450,
        889.6875
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 1,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "Note: how to run this experiment",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "# Prompt-generation experiment\n\nThis graph exists to answer one question: **does a local vision-language model write better H3 prompts than we do by hand?** It is the shipped ref2v turbo recipe with nothing changed except what the experiment needs, so a result here transfers directly to the graphs you already use.\n\n## The rule that makes it an experiment\n\n**The seed is fixed at 1234 and must stay fixed.** Everything else is held too: same references, same 0.6 MP, same 6 steps, same shift. The prompt text is the only variable. The moment the seed moves you are comparing two different rolls of the dice and no amount of watching will separate the prompt's contribution from luck.\n\nIf you change resolution or duration, change it for every arm and start the comparison over.\n\n## The three arms\n\n| Arm | Where the prompt comes from |\n|---|---|\n| **control** | written by hand - the text this node ships with |\n| **writer** | the H3 Prompt Writer extension, floating button or Extensions menu |\n| **raw** | the same model asked directly, with a community system prompt |\n\nThe *writer* arm wraps the model in H3-specific scaffolding, reads your reference images itself and repairs its own output. The *raw* arm is one system prompt and a brief. They are not obviously ordered - that is the point of running both.\n\nPaste each into the prompt node, run, and keep the file. Outputs land under `output/video/promptgen/`.\n\n## Before you can run either\n\nThe prompt model is not loaded by ComfyUI and does not live in this graph. Start it from a terminal:\n\n```\nbash /app/scripts/promptgen.sh start\n```\n\nFirst run downloads about 20 GB onto the volume and takes a while. After that it is seconds.\n\n## The part that will bite you\n\n**The two models do not fit on the card at the same time.** H3 is 21 GB of diffusion plus 15.7 GB of text encoder; the prompt model is another 18. On 32 GB that is not close.\n\nSo the loop is: write prompts, unload the writer, render. The Writer's **Keep model loaded** switch off, or `promptgen.sh unload`, frees the card. Skip that and the render will either crawl through offload or fail outright - and it will look like an H3 problem.\n\n---\n\n## About the underlying graph\n\n[MiniMax H3](https://www.minimax.io/blog/minimax-h3) is MiniMax's general-purpose, omni-modal generation model. It jointly understands text, image, video, and audio, and generates video with **native stereo audio**: voice, sound effects, and music are modeled jointly in a single forward pass, not layered on afterward. Output is up to 2K resolution, 24fps, and up to about 15 seconds.\n\n## ComfyUI links\n- [ComfyUI#15224](https://github.com/Comfy-Org/ComfyUI/pull/15224)\n- [🤗 Comfy-Org/MiniMax-H3](https://huggingface.co/Comfy-Org/MiniMax-H3)\n\n## About this workflow\n\nThis template runs the **reference-to-video (ref2va)** task using the `MiniMaxH3ReferenceToVideo` node. It takes any mix of reference images, videos, and standalone audio, and weaves them into the generation to lock in a character's identity, a style, a motion, a camera move, or a voice.\n\n**Key inputs**\n\n- **ref_images / ref_videos / ref_video_audios / ref_audios**: up to 9 reference images, 3 reference videos (each may carry its own paired soundtrack), and 3 standalone reference audio clips\n- **prompt**: reference the inputs by tag, in the exact order they were connected, for example `<Picture 1>`, `<Video 1>`, `<Audio 1>`, then describe the target scene, motion, and audio\n- **ref_image_size**: `match` scales references down to the generation's resolution (faster); `max` keeps up to a 2048px short edge for stronger identity fidelity, at the cost of speed since reference tokens ride along every sampling step\n- **width / height**: set via Resolution Selector.\n- **duration (seconds)**: converted to a valid frame `length` by the Math Expression node\n\n**Sampling and decode**\n\n- Sampler: `res_multistep`. `beta` or `normal` scheduler tends to outperform `simple` for reference-heavy prompts like this one\n- The sampler's joint audio+video `LATENT` output feeds directly into both `VAEDecode` (video, `minimax_h3_video_vae_fp16`) and `VAEDecodeAudio` (audio, `minimax_h3_audio_vae_fp32`); each decode node automatically pulls its own half out of the packed latent. `CreateVideo` then muxes the two into a single MP4 with synced sound\n- The diffusion model here is `minimax_h3_ref2va_pruned_int8_convrot.safetensors`, a different set of weights from the `fl2va` model used by the t2v/i2v templates\n\nRef2va's output is very sensitive to prompt wording; matching the reference tags precisely and being explicit about which reference drives which part of the shot tends to work best."
      ],
      "color": "#222",
      "bgcolor": "#000"
    },
